43","ff6ce1d27fdf6ae7","ff6d4d7320e508a6","ff975087cca7dc91","fffbd6c1d9a07a59"],"groups":["dbcaf7ad9a55de2c","a999e75127b541ac","66fefc98e552343f","e2b6cd39972d6747","c236f9638e0aaece","23d2db520e2240ab","dd84f6c39b19663f","866a63cf7383893a","f63b4ba73263e9c1","d2bf518467bb5749","c67eb019272ef157","3c5f23b25c8619d5","2a9d34b34fe2217f","bbf6e50d02ed4845","c92e2b6194adadc0","a2d0ecdb69f60d29","04737ab170a026c6","94a24a3704db0500","3492b961a8bec34e","622ce0918dfbff8b"],"findings":"## Review of the signals / floor / derived-attention change\n\nI ran the full suite in the clone: `npm test` gives 167 pass, 0 fail, and the 900-hunk performance test takes 41 ms. I also ran `signals HEAD~1..HEAD` on the snapshot. The annotations are mostly accurate, and the groups I checked against the code and tests hold up. The issues below are small.\n\n**1. Mismatch (read): the built-in defaults are not language-free.** Group `config` says the defaults hold \"only conventions true almost everywhere, no language, framework or build tool beyond well-known file names\", and the README repeats \"name no language, framework or tool, only common file names\". But `DEFAULTS.patterns.sensitive` includes `\\b(DROP|TRUNCATE|ALTER)\\s+TABLE\\b` (SQL-specific), and the `paths` defaults name specific ecosystems (Cargo, go, Python, Docker, Jenkins). The file names are arguably covered by \"well-known file names\", but the SQL regex is not. Either the wording or the default should change.\n\n**2. Mismatch (verified): the commit's own config makes the whole change must-read.** Group `config` notes that the sensitive floor comes from the default secret and encryption patterns. I ran `signals HEAD~1..HEAD --json`: the floor is `sensitive`/`must-read`, and the only raising hunk is `h18 src/config.ts`, at lines 45, 65 and 66 (`secret or credential`, `encryption`). So the author's own gloss of this change must either declare `must-read` or give an `override_reason`. The risk list mentions noise on code that lists these patterns, but not this consequence for the change itself. Accurate otherwise: the note and the risk are both correct about the cause.\n\n**3. Mismatch, comment only (read): `pathFloor` in `src/signals.ts`.** The comment says \"test, docs, generated or manifest-in-test: capped at local\". There is no manifest-specific rule. A manifest under a test path is capped at local only because the `test` class caps it. The behaviour is right; the comment names a rule the code does not have.\n\n**4. Missing risk (read): `add` gates on the working-tree config, not the commit's.** The risk list says `show` and `html` of an old commit can change after a config edit. `add` also calls `loadConfig()`, which reads the current checkout's `.gloss/config.json`. So a config edit can turn a previously acceptable gloss into an `add` error. This is the same mechanism, but `add` is a write gate, so the consequence is more serious than the one listed.\n\n**5. Misgrading (read, uncertain): `glob` as `supporting`, with the reason \"missing on Node 20\".** The annotation is directionally right, since `path.matchesGlob` is recent and `engines` is `>=20.12`. I did not verify its availability on 20.12–20.16 (this machine runs Node 26), so the stated reason is unconfirmed. The group is needed either way, because `engines` does not allow requiring Node 22.\n\n**6. Simplification (read, low value):** `analyze()` in `src/signals.ts` maintains file floor and `why` with a three-way branch that could be a single reduce over the file's hunk floors, using `maxImpact` and the existing `partFloor` pattern. This is optional; the current logic is tested.\n\n**Checked and accurate (read, with test evidence where noted):**\n- Pattern hits use only added and removed lines, never context or the `@@` header. The tests cover this.\n- The `g` and `y` flag stripping, verified by the test suite.\n- Lockfile and test/docs/generated floor rules match `pathFloor`.\n- The derivation rules (one raise step, test-s