7715e0579a511","3492b961a8bec34e","622ce0918dfbff8b"],"findings":"## Review of the phase 2 signals and floor change\n\nTwo mismatches are confirmed by running code. Most other checks are from reading only.\n\n### 1. Mismatches\n\n- **Manifest hunks are not \"inert\", contrary to the code's own comments.** The `Hunk.inert` comment in `src/signals.ts` says \"dependency files\" don't change behaviour. The README and the `floor-derive` notes say the same. But `inert` is `test || docs || generated || lockfile`, so a `package.json` hunk is not inert. Running `hunkSignals` on a `package.json` version edit gave `inert: false, classes: contract,manifest`. **Verified.** The effect is that a `required` group made only of manifest edits counts as behaviour-changing and can raise derived attention. Either the code or the documented rule needs changing. (`signals-engine`, `floor-derive`, `readme`)\n- **A user regex with the `g` flag misses hits.** `mergeConfig` compiles user patterns with their flags and `hunkSignals` calls `p.re.test(...)`. With `flags: \"g\"`, `lastIndex` carries over between lines. Three added lines containing `device.write(` gave 2 hits, not 3. **Verified.** `RepoConfig` only checks that the flags compile, so `g` and `y` are accepted. The fix is to strip `g` and `y` or to reset `lastIndex`. (`config`, `signals-engine`)\n- **`html` shows derived impact badges on every undeclared group, even in plain glosses.** `commitData` sets `impact_derived` whenever `g.impact` is absent, and `html` draws it with no threshold. `show` hides the impact column unless a floor is above `module`, so the two views disagree. **Read.** (`html-display`, `show-display`)\n\n### 2. Misgraded necessity\n\n- `add-checks` is labelled `required` as a whole, but `--allow-underrate` is not needed. `override_reason` already covers a justified under-rating. The flag lets an author bypass the attention floor without giving any reason, which works against the skill's \"don't reach for --allow-underrate\" guidance. **Read.** Its test exists, so it is not dead code, but it is a speculative extra inside a required group.\n- `signals-command`'s `workingDiff` (`git.ts`) is labelled `required` but is really a supporting group for `signals wip`. It is part of the `signals-command` group, so this is a small point. **Read.**\n- The `loader-hunks`, `glob`, `signals-tests` and `test-updates` groups are correctly graded as `supporting`.\n\n### 3. Removal candidates\n\n- `--allow-underrate` (in `add-checks`). Dropping it costs nothing the task needs, since `override_reason` covers the justified case. Impact: one flag and one test.\n- The `performance` test in `signals-tests` only logs a timing and is weakly tied to any stated behaviour. It can stay if the author wants a regression guard, but it is not needed for the task.\n\n### 4. Simplifications\n\n- `inert` and `behaviour` repeat the same \"is this test/docs/generated/lockfile\" set. Derive one from the other, which also fixes the manifest mismatch above.\n- `floorFrom` computes `why` in a way that is hard to follow. It would read more simply as two cases: path wins (keep path reasons) or hits win (only pattern names).\n\n### 5. Missing risks\n\n- The `g`/`y` regex flags issue above is not listed by the author.\n- The author lists config read from the working tree, but not that `show` and `html` of old commits silently change their derived values when the config changes. This is partly covered by the \"computed on read\" note, but the stale-config case for old commits is worth stating.\n- The `hunks` summary calls `loadConfig` through `withSignals`, which prints the malformed-config warning once per process. In `show` on a repo with a broken config, the output is \"unrated\" without the reason. This is only visible on stderr.\n- I did not run the full test suite, and I did not check the HTML page in a browser. The findings above come from targeted runs of `config`/`signals` and from reading the rest.\n\nScratch scrip